    "updated_at": "2017-05-18T15:48:41Z",
    "body": "OK, so stepping through the code above, it does the following, in order:\r\n\r\n1. Create a frame.\r\n2. Set its src to `/common/blank.html`.\r\n3. Wait for that to load.\r\n4. Set the src to `/`.\r\n5. Add a beforeunload listener.\r\n6. Add an unload listener.\r\n\r\nNote that the beforeunload listener is added _after_ the src set, as @mystor notes.  This part is key.\r\n\r\nPer spec, https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#dom-iframe-src calls into https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#process-the-iframe-attributes which in this case does https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#otherwise-steps-for-iframe-or-frame-elements and that calls into <https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#navigate>.  All of this happens synchronously, right?\r\n\r\nNow in navigate, step 7 is \"prompt to unload\" and fires the beforeunload event, still sync under the src set.  And this happens before the beforeunload listener is added.\r\n\r\nSo as far as I can tell, Firefox is following the spec here.  I don't know what other browsers are doing (e.g. whether they are firing beforeunload async from navigation, or doing navigation async from src sets, or something else that causes the beforeunload to fire after the listener is added).",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
